  "query_preview": "..\nThis afternoon and tonight through Monday: The Inland NW will \nsee evolving rain chances this period, along with a risk \nembedded thunderstorms, and a marginal risk (5-14% chance per \nWPC) for excessive rainfall over Idaho and the far eastern \nfringes of WA late Sunday into Monday morning. Tonight a broad \nlow, centered near the Oregon coast, is impacting much of the \nPacific Northwest. The south to southeast flow into our area \nwill work with daytime instability and an impulse riding north \nto bring chances for showers and isolated thunderstorms through \nsunset. The best chances will remain near the Cascades to north-\ncentral WA, with pops near 20-59%. Secondary chances will be \nfound from near the Blue Mountains north toward the Spokane/CdA \narea and northeast WA/N ID mountains, with pops around 10-20%. \nAfter about 8-9 PM the risk will weaken, lingering near the \nnorth to Cascades to north-central WA. Other chances start to \nback into the ID/MT border overnight as the broad low starts to \nmove. Lows will largely be in the 40s, with some low 50s in the \nL-C valley and near the Cascades. Some patchy fog is also \npossible again heading into Sunday morning in the sheltered \nnortheast valleys and higher Palouse.\n\nThen heading into Sunday and Monday the offshore low moves east\nacross Oregon and toward the lower ID Panhandle. This will work\nwith a shifting deformation axis and deepening PWATs over \nsoutheast WA through the ID Panhandle to bring increasing \nprecipitation chances. Chances remain limited to the ID/MT \nborder in Sunday morning, then start expanding to eastern WA in\nthe afternoon and eventually south-central WA by Sunday evening.\nPoPs rises to 60-90% over southeast/east-central WA and central\nand southern ID Panhandle. Within this region is also increasing\nconvective instability with CAPE values between averaging \nbetween 500-1000 J/kg. Forecast soundings are modestly saturated\nthis period and skinny CAPE, with supports locally heavier rain\npotential rather than large hail or strong winds. \n\nThe WPC has marginal risk for excessive rainfall over the WA \ncounties along the ID border and across the ID Panhandle for \nSunday through early Monday. So we will have to watch for debris\nflows near burn scars, rock slides, and rises on small creeks \nand streams. The PWATs in that region sit between 0.75 to 1.0 \ninches. The higher probabilities are across the central and \nsouthern ID Panhandle, where 24-hour probabilities from 5 PM \nSunday to 5 PM Monday for 0.50 inches are between 70-90%, 60-80%\nfor 0.75 inches and 30-60% for 1 inch. As we head into Monday \nevening and overnight the low starts to shift east and the \nprecipitation potential starts to wane. By the overnight the \nbest chances linger over the north and central ID Panhandle, \nbleeding a bit west into the northeast WA mountains, with PoPs \naround 40-50% and slight chances into the Spokane areas. \n\nAs the low is starting to exit Monday winds will become breezy \nthrough the day and early evening. West-southwest winds of 10-15\nmph and gusts of 20-25 mph will be possible. Highs will be in \nthe 70s Sunday, then the 60s and 70s Monday with those cooler \nreadings over the east third of WA and ID under the higher rain \nchances. Lows will be in the 40s to lower 50s.\n\nTuesday to Saturday: The Inland NW transitions to a drier \npattern with warming temperatures, before additional rain \nchances arrive Friday into Saturday. A weak ridge to zonal \npattern develops Tuesday into Thursday, leading to the warming \ntemperatures that pushes highs into the 70s to low 80s \nWednesday, reaching the upper 70s to upper 80s by Thursday. A \ntrough the drops into toward the end of the week, bringing those\nrain chances with highs dropping back to the upper 60s to upper\n70s by next Saturday. /Solveig",
